INT. MERCY GENERAL - HOSPICE WING WAITING AREA - NIGHT

Two vinyl sofas. A fish tank with one fish. A TV on mute, a weather map. A vending machine with a taped sign: "NO REFUNDS." A door to the room beyond, shut.

The Okonjo family, six of them, arranged like pieces on a board.

CHIDI OKONJO, 60s, the eldest son, sits upright with his coat on. NGOZI OKONJO, 50s, his sister, fidgets with her rings. EMEKA OKONJO, 40s, the middle brother, leans on the wall with his arms crossed. ADAEZE OKONJO, 30s, the youngest, holds a Styrofoam cup of coffee she isn't drinking. TUNDE, 30s, her husband, studies his phone. And GRANDMOTHER ADA's old friend MRS. ETIM, 70s, in her church hat, folds a paper napkin into smaller and smaller squares.

Nobody speaks.

Chidi checks his watch. Ngozi checks hers. Emeka looks at the door. Adaeze looks at Tunde. Tunde looks at his phone. Mrs. Etim looks at the fish. The fish looks at nothing.

CHIDI
It has been an hour.

NGOZI
It has been fifty-two minutes.

CHIDI
Fifty-two is an hour.

NGOZI
Fifty-two is fifty-two.

EMEKA
Please.

Adaeze offers the coffee to no one in particular. Tunde takes it. Sips. Makes a face. Hands it back.

TUNDE
It's cold.

ADAEZE
I know.

Mrs. Etim unfolds the napkin, smooths it flat on her knee, folds it again.

MRS. ETIM
She asked for jollof last Sunday.

NGOZI
She asked for jollof every Sunday.

MRS. ETIM
This one she ate.

EMEKA ^
She ate it.

ADAEZE ^
All of it.

Silence. The weather map changes colors. Nobody looks at the TV. The door opens and a NURSE's hand rests on the frame, then the door closes again.

Everyone stands at once. Chidi, then Ngozi, then Emeka, then Adaeze and Tunde, then Mrs. Etim leaning on her cane. They stand in a half-circle facing the door. It does not open again. No one sits.

CHIDI
(quietly)
We wait.
